WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.860
Do you have the same color eyes as your mom or the same hair color as your dad?

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.710
Just by observing our appearance,

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.320
we get a sense that some traits are heritable.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.840
They get passed on from parent to offspring.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.180
But how does heritable information get transmitted from one generation to the next?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.340
In the 1940s,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.710
this was a complete mystery.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.710
We are going to tell you the story of three scientists Oswald Avery,

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.160
Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty who set out to solve this puzzle.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:36.720
Their pioneering research provided proof that deoxyribonucleic acid,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:37.540
or DNA,

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.250
contains the blueprint of who we are and what we look like.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:42.630
That DNA is,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:43.200
in fact,

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.560
the heritable information.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.250
This finding may seem obvious now,

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:48.520
but back in the 1940s,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.200
it was incredibly controversial.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.100
Most scientists thought that DNA was too simple to contain all of the information for 

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.960
every single heritable trait,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.490
and instead they thought proteins,

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.780
which are large and complex molecules,

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.820
must be the substance passed on from parent to child.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.880
At the start of our story, our three scientists wanted to follow up on an important 

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.570
finding from a scientist named Frederick Griffith.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.090
Griffith worked with a type of bacteria that came in two strains,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.150
one that looked smooth and one that looked rough.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.610
If he injected the smooth strain into mice,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:27.300
they died.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.480
But if he injected the rough,

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.300
strain the mice lived.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.750
To his surprise, if he first killed the smooth bacteria,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.600
these dead bacteria did not kill the mice.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.910
But if he injected a mixture of dead,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.530
smooth bacteria and live rough bacteria,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.820
the mice died.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.020
This meant that, amazingly,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.420
something from the smooth strain was transferred to and permanently 

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.490
inherited by the rough strain,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.050
a process he called transformation,

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.550
and this caused the rough bacteria to become deadly.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.050
This exciting finding left a big puzzle that Avery, MacLeod and 

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.790
McCarty wanted to solve.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.740
What was the inheritable information that caused the transformation of the 

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.180
rough strain into the deadly, smooth bacteria.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.750
The scientist purified a chemical substance from the smooth bacteria,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.810
and they repeated Griffith's results with it. When they applied the substance to 

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.020
the rough bacteria,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.450
those rough bacteria transformed into smooth bacteria,

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.750
and this trait was heritable and permanent.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:39.230
But the question still remained, what type of molecule was this heritable 

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:39.980
information?

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.700
At the time,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:42.430
most people,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.490
including Avery,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.390
thought the answer was protein.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.630
But they found that enzymes that destroyed proteins could not block the 

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.050
activity of the substance.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.200
It still made rough bacteria look smooth.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.720
This meant the substance was not protein,

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.500
so could it be DNA instead?

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:06.370
When they added an enzyme that destroys DNA, but does not destroy protein,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.050
the transforming activity disappeared.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.260
Avery and colleagues confirmed that the heritable information had a chemical 

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.840
signature that matched DNA and that their preparation had very little 

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.500
contaminating protein or other molecules.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.110
The researchers came to the surprising conclusion that DNA was,

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:26.910
in fact,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.260
the heritable information.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.010
Despite the beautiful evidence from these experiments,

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:37.150
many scientists questioned whether some contaminating protein might account for 

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:37.840
the results.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.300
It took another experiment by Hershey and Chase in 1952, described in 

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.840
another video, and the discovery of the double helical structure of DNA in 

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.690
1953 to convince everyone beyond any doubt that DNA 

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.450
is the molecule of heredity.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.670
Once people were convinced that DNA was the heritable information,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.880
it sparked a wave of new research questions that served as the foundation 

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.590
for biotechnology, revolutionizing modern medicine.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.290
Many new treatments for human diseases rely on our ability to work with 

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.650
DNA and even pass genetic information from organism to organism.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.980
One impactful example is in treating patients with type 1 diabetes who require 

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.680
daily insulin injections.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.130
Previously,

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.300
insulin from livestock was used.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:33.340
But now, human insulin DNA is transferred to bacteria,

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.760
which act as biological factories to produce human insulin for patients.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.370
So the early discovery of DNA transformation by Avery, MacLeod and McCarty 

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.300
has had long lasting benefits for humankind.

